                <details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"><summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Is mouth breathing during sleep harmful?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary><div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Chronic mouth breathing can reduce nitric oxide production, cause dry mouth/throat, contribute to snoring, and may indicate underlying sleep apnea.</div></details>
                <details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"><summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">How can I stop breathing through my mouth at night?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary><div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Treatment depends on the cause. Options include nasal sprays, allergy treatment, mouth taping, or surgical correction of nasal obstruction.</div></details>
                <details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"><summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Can surgery help with mouth breathing?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary><div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Yes, septoplasty and turbinate reduction can correct structural causes of nasal obstruction, enabling comfortable nasal breathing during sleep.</div></details>
                <details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"><summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Does mouth breathing indicate sleep apnea?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary><div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Not always, but mouth breathing can be a sign of airway obstruction. A sleep study can determine if sleep apnea is present.</div></details>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>
